    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class (`AcceptanceBase` is abstract, `AuditLogCapture` is a helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on owner creation. When an owner is created via `POST /api/owners`, the response (and subsequent `GET /api/owners/{id}`) now reports how many owners already existed sharing the same first and last name, case-insensitively.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` (integer, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a persisted `namesake_count` column with getter/setter, mirroring the existing `householdId` computed field. MapStruct maps it into the DTO automatically.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 In `addOwner`, compute the count before saving via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper, which counts existing owners whose first *and* last names match case-insensitively (reusing the existing `normalizeHouseholdField` trim/collapse/lowercase normalization used by the household rule). Because it runs before `saveOwner`, the new owner never counts itself.\n\n4. **DB schema** \u2014 Added the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) and appended the trailing value to the positional owner `INSERT`s in `hsqldb/data.sql` (the DB the tests use). h2/postgres use named-column inserts and mysql uses `INSERT IGNORE`, so those data files needed no change.\n\nIn the test, two owners share `firstName`/`lastName` but have distinct addresses (so they don't collide on the household rule); the second owner correctly reports `namesakeCount = 1`.",
